          %h2#References References
          %h3#historical_arguments Historical arguments
          %p Discussion by the founders of quantum mechanics includes the following.
          %p In
          %ul
            %li#EinsteinPodoldskyRosen35
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+Einstein"}> Albert Einstein
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boris+Podolsky"} Boris Podolsky
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nathan+Rosen"} Nathan Rosen
              %em Can the Quantum-Mechanical Description of Physical Reality be Considered Complete?
              Physical Review
              %strong 47
              10 (1935) 777-780 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRev.47.777"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRev.47.777
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            it was argued (see at
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/EPR+paradox"} EPR paradox
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nils+Bohr"}> Nils Bohr
              ,
              %em Can Quantum-Mechanical Description of Physical Reality be Considered Complete?
              Physical Review 48, 696–702 (1935) (
              %a{:href => "http://prola.aps.org/abstract/PR/v48/i8/p696_1"}> publisher
              )
          %p
            The famous assertion (pp. 209) by Bohr that all experiments in quantum mechanics must be possible to describe in “classical terms” (cf.
            = succeed "):" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+topos"} Bohr topos
          %ul
            %li#Bohr49
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nils+Bohr"}> Nils Bohr
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Discussion with Einstein on Epistemological Problems in Atomic Physics
              = succeed "," do
                %em Albert Einstein, Philosopher-Scientist
              %strong VII
              Evanston (1949) 201-241, and
              %em Niels Bohr Collected Works
              %strong 7
              (1996) 339-381 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S1876-0503(08)70379-7"}> doi:10.1016/S1876-0503(08)70379-7
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p#PrimasStatementOfCopenhagen
            The first systematic statement of what should count as the
            %em Copenhagen interpretation
            (broadly attributed to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Werner+Heisenberg"} Werner Heisenberg
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nils+Bohr"} Nils Bohr
            %a{:href => "#Primas83"} Primas (1983) p. 99
            (reproduced in
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#Omnès94"} Omnès (1994) p. 85
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/Primas-CopenhagenInterpretation.jpg", :width => "600"}/
          %p
            The
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/many-worlds+interpretation+of+quantum+mechanics"} many-worlds interpretation of quantum mechanics
          %ul
            %li#Everett57a
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hugh+Everett+III"}> Hugh Everett III
                ,
                %em The Theory of the Universal Wavefunction
                PhD thesis, Princeton (1957) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/nova/manyworlds/pdf/dissertation.pdf"}> pdf
                \&amp;rbrack; reprinted in:
                %a{:href => "#DeWittGraham73"} De Witt &amp; Graham 1973
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (for discussion of the key argument here see at
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deferred+measurement+principle"} deferred measurement principle
                  the section
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %em
                      %a{:href => "deferred+measurement+principle#DeferredMeasurementAndInterpretations"} Deferred measurement and Interpretations
            %li#Everett57b
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hugh+Everett+III"}> Hugh Everett III
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em “Relative State” Formulation of Quantum Mechanics
                %strong 29
                (1957) 454-462 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/RevModPhys.29.454"}> doi:10.1103/RevModPhys.29.454
                \&amp;rbrack; (reprinted in
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#DeWittGraham73"} De Witt &amp; Graham 1973
              %blockquote
                %p “This total lack of effect of one branch on another also implies that no observer will ever be aware of any ‘splitting’ process. Arguments that the world picture presented by this theory is contradicted by experience, because we are unaware of any branching process, are like the criticism of the Copernican theory that the mobility of the earth as a real physical fact is incompatible with the common sense interpretation of nature because we feel no such motion. In both cases the argument fails when it is shown that the theory itself predicts that our experience will be what it in fact is.”
            %li#DeWittGraham73
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bryce+S.+DeWitt"}> Bryce S. DeWitt
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Neill+Graham"} Neill Graham
                (eds.),
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Many-Worlds Interpretation of Quantum Mechanics
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://press.princeton.edu/books/hardcover/9780691645926/the-many-worlds-interpretation-of-quantum-mechanics"} ISBN:9780691645926
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctt13x0wwk"} jstor:j.ctt13x0wwk
          %p The catch-phrase “shut up and calculate” for the agnostic interpretation is due to
          %ul
